  "query_preview": "..\n\nNext 7 Days...\n\nThe forecast for Friday and Saturday remains roughly the same as\nwhat we have been seeing for the past 1 to 2 days. A cold front is\nforecast to move through the CWA this morning which will bring \nwith it our first taste of fall. In addition to the notable \nincrease in moisture, the high temperatures today will be roughly \n10 degrees below average for most locations, or 20 degrees colder \nthan what we saw Thursday. Just behind this FROPA, and lingering \nthrough the day Friday, there will be scattered showers and \nthunderstorms. The highest coverage of precip will be in the \nmountains and foothills of south-centeral Montana and decreasing \nin coverage with eastward extent. Saturday will be fairly similar \nto Friday with temps 10 degrees below average and isolated showers\nacross the region. Coverage of precipitation will decrease through\nthe day leading to widely isolated showers by evening. \n\nOver the past day or so models have trended the Sunday shortwave \nfarther to the north. This would result in warmer and drier \nconditions then previously forecast. At one point there was a few\ninches of snow forecast above 9kft and a high chance of a wetting\nrain across the entire CWA Sunday, there is now a less than a 50%\nchance region-wide for a wetting rain Sunday. With this more \nnortherly track of the low now, we will actually see a slight temperature\nrebound on Sunday. There is still a large model spread on how \nmuch temps will rebound, but highs near average are possible \nbefore another push of cool air to start the work week. \n\nWith parts of our CWA now in the \"dry slot\" of the low on Sunday,\nthere is a slight chance that some stronger thunderstorms may \ndevelop in the eastern portion of the CWA. During the afternoon \nand evening Sunday, a cold front will push from west to east \nacross the area acting the the primary source of lift for any \nconvection. Due to the cooler temps, modeled MU-CAPE values remain\nunder 1000 J/Kg, however this would be plenty sufficient given \nthe strong source of lift aiding CI as the front pushes through\nlate afternoon and evening. Additionally, shear values in SE \nMontana will be in the 30-50kt range due to the proximity and \nlocation of the upper level low. In terms of moisture, PWAT values\nare forecast to exceed 1\" in the eastern 1/3 of the CWA ahead of \nthe FROPA which will be plenty for thunderstorm development. \nWith this event just now being depicted on models, plus it being \nseveral days away, there is still several questions surrounding \nthe event. The few CAMs that run out that far are depicting some \nstrong storms in NE WY and just clipping SE Montana. This signal \nwill be monitored as we get closer to the potential event.\n\nTo start the work week we will remain under the main branch of \nthe jet stream which will allow for near to just below average \ntemperatures to linger through the first part of the week. There \nis a chance that isolated showers continue with these cooler \ntemperatures due to the strong jet dynamics overhead. WMR",
